1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,620
The last B-52 left the factory in 1962.

2
00:00:04,491 --> 00:00:08,291
In some American families,
three generations have flown in it.

3
00:00:08,831 --> 00:00:10,531
From grandfather to grandson.

4
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:15,550
And the US Air Force
plans to keep flying it into the 2050s.

5
00:00:15,962 --> 00:00:19,162
That's close to a hundred years
after its first flight.

6
00:00:19,898 --> 00:00:23,738
Along the way, it has outlived
the bomber that was meant to replace it,

7
00:00:23,898 --> 00:00:26,958
a giant that flew at three times
the speed of sound.

8
00:00:27,267 --> 00:00:30,727
And it's expected to outlive
the B-2 stealth bomber, too.

9
00:00:31,774 --> 00:00:36,294
So why does the most powerful air force in
the world keep flying its oldest bomber?

10
00:00:38,134 --> 00:00:40,354
Here's the first part of the answer.

11
00:00:41,110 --> 00:00:45,350
Every one of its would-be replacements
was built to be the best at something.

12
00:00:45,985 --> 00:00:49,005
Faster. Higher. Harder to see on radar.

13
00:00:49,815 --> 00:00:53,895
The B-52 was built
to carry a lot of weight a very long way.

14
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:57,735
And being the best at something
turns out to have an expiry date.

15
00:00:58,405 --> 00:01:00,845
Carrying a lot, a long way, doesn't.

16
00:01:02,915 --> 00:01:04,995
But that's only half the answer.

17
00:01:05,741 --> 00:01:09,841
A job that never goes away
is no use if the plane wears out.

18
00:01:10,203 --> 00:01:13,723
And the B-52 has had
three completely different jobs.

19
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:19,000
To see how it survived all three, we need
to go back to a hotel room in Ohio.

20
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:25,600
October 1948. Dayton, Ohio.

21
00:01:26,487 --> 00:01:29,407
A team of Boeing engineers
had come to show the Air Force

22
00:01:29,637 --> 00:01:31,367
their design for a new bomber.

23
00:01:31,959 --> 00:01:33,159
It had propellers.

24
00:01:33,239 --> 00:01:36,299
And the Air Force
wanted to talk about jets.

25
00:01:37,135 --> 00:01:42,155
So over one weekend, in the Hotel Van
Cleve, the engineers redesigned it.

26
00:01:42,671 --> 00:01:44,871
Swept wings. Eight jet engines.

27
00:01:45,732 --> 00:01:49,932
One of them went to a hobby shop, bought
some balsa wood, and carved a model.

28
00:01:50,543 --> 00:01:54,003
On Monday, they handed over
a thirty-three-page proposal

29
00:01:54,256 --> 00:01:56,676
and a model you could hold in one hand.

30
00:01:57,381 --> 00:01:58,961
That model became the B-52.

31
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,560
It first flew in 1952.

32
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:07,520
By 1962, Boeing had built
seven hundred and forty-four of them.

33
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:12,330
Its first job was simple, and terrible.

34
00:02:13,061 --> 00:02:17,861
Fly very high, very far, into the
Soviet Union, and drop nuclear bombs.

35
00:02:18,597 --> 00:02:20,537
Altitude was its protection.

36
00:02:21,466 --> 00:02:26,886
Then, in 1960, a Soviet missile shot down
an American U-2 spy plane,

37
00:02:27,078 --> 00:02:29,878
flying far higher than the B-52 could.

38
00:02:30,566 --> 00:02:32,926
Flying high had stopped being safe.

39
00:02:35,196 --> 00:02:39,616
And that giant, three times the speed of
sound, was already on the drawing board.

40
00:02:40,456 --> 00:02:41,656
The Valkyrie.

41
00:02:42,035 --> 00:02:44,035
It was designed to cross
into Soviet airspace

42
00:02:44,265 --> 00:02:48,295
more than twenty kilometres up,
faster than a rifle bullet.

43
00:02:49,022 --> 00:02:52,542
The perfect answer to a question
that had just stopped mattering.

44
00:02:52,802 --> 00:02:54,822
Only two were ever built.

45
00:02:57,202 --> 00:03:01,002
The B-52 did something else. It went low.

46
00:03:01,737 --> 00:03:06,637
Crews trained to fly it a few hundred feet
above the ground, under the enemy's radar.

47
00:03:07,585 --> 00:03:08,905
Job number two.

48
00:03:09,673 --> 00:03:11,513
It wasn't designed for that.

49
00:03:11,973 --> 00:03:15,873
The air near the ground is rough,
and a big wing gets shaken for hours.

50
00:03:16,598 --> 00:03:20,338
On one test flight in 1964,
over the Rocky Mountains,

51
00:03:20,418 --> 00:03:23,948
turbulence tore most of the tail fin
off a B-52.

52
00:03:24,338 --> 00:03:26,198
The crew still landed it.

53
00:03:28,318 --> 00:03:32,618
And by the 1970s, even flying low
was getting dangerous.

54
00:03:33,121 --> 00:03:36,301
So in 1977, the United States cancelled

55
00:03:36,531 --> 00:03:40,521
the first version of the B-1,
the plane meant to replace the B-52.

56
00:03:41,835 --> 00:03:45,695
One big reason was a new weapon.
The cruise missile.

57
00:03:46,413 --> 00:03:49,393
A cruise missile flies itself,
low and alone,

58
00:03:49,473 --> 00:03:51,804
for more than two thousand kilometres.

59
00:03:51,884 --> 00:03:55,184
So the bomber no longer had to get
anywhere near its target.

60
00:03:55,927 --> 00:03:59,947
It just had to carry the missiles
to the edge of the map, and let go.

61
00:04:00,631 --> 00:04:02,111
Job number three.

62
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:05,960
And for this job,
the B-52 was almost perfect.

63
00:04:06,681 --> 00:04:09,241
It can carry up to twenty
of those missiles.

64
00:04:10,041 --> 00:04:11,761
It didn't need to be fast.

65
00:04:11,961 --> 00:04:14,121
It didn't need to be invisible.

66
00:04:14,918 --> 00:04:17,798
It needed to be big,
and to fly a long way.

67
00:04:18,027 --> 00:04:22,427
Which is exactly what those engineers
in Dayton had drawn in 1948.

68
00:04:23,435 --> 00:04:25,475
Three completely different jobs.

69
00:04:26,126 --> 00:04:28,786
And all three needed
the same thing from the plane.

70
00:04:31,856 --> 00:04:34,596
But even the perfect truck
wears out eventually.

71
00:04:35,144 --> 00:04:39,404
So how is a plane built in the early 1960s
still flying at all?

72
00:04:40,133 --> 00:04:43,873
Because a wing wears out
mostly by flying, not by the calendar.

73
00:04:44,645 --> 00:04:47,985
Every take-off, every gust,
every landing flexes it.

74
00:04:48,065 --> 00:04:50,225
And the metal keeps count.

75
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:55,190
A busy airliner can fly three or four
thousand hours in a single year.

76
00:04:55,698 --> 00:04:58,218
A B-52 flies a few hundred.

77
00:04:58,951 --> 00:05:02,831
For most of the Cold War,
part of the fleet wasn't flying at all.

78
00:05:03,399 --> 00:05:06,879
It sat on alert, at the end of the runway,
fuelled and armed,

79
00:05:07,006 --> 00:05:10,546
with crews sleeping nearby,
ready to take off within minutes.

80
00:05:11,262 --> 00:05:13,102
Waiting barely wears out a wing.

81
00:05:13,182 --> 00:05:14,422
Flying does.

82
00:05:16,734 --> 00:05:22,034
By 2016, the average B-52 had flown
about eighteen thousand hours.

83
00:05:22,718 --> 00:05:26,823
Its wings were estimated
to last at least thirty-two thousand.

84
00:05:26,923 --> 00:05:29,823
So by the measure that matters,
the average B-52

85
00:05:29,903 --> 00:05:32,443
had used up only about half its wing.

86
00:05:35,223 --> 00:05:40,083
And when a fire wrecked one in 2014,
the Air Force didn't order a new one.

87
00:05:40,289 --> 00:05:42,809
Instead, it went to the Arizona desert,

88
00:05:43,085 --> 00:05:46,865
picked a B-52 that had been
sitting in the sun for seven years,

89
00:05:47,033 --> 00:05:49,233
rebuilt it, and sent it back to work.

90
00:05:49,813 --> 00:05:51,673
Its nickname is Ghost Rider.

91
00:05:52,443 --> 00:05:55,023
A few years later, a second one followed.

92
00:05:55,717 --> 00:05:57,297
It helps that so many were built.

93
00:05:57,527 --> 00:06:00,656
Seven hundred and forty-four.

94
00:06:00,957 --> 00:06:03,157
Of the B-2,
there were only ever twenty-one.

95
00:06:05,802 --> 00:06:09,662
And the B-52 is the cheapest bomber
for the Air Force to fly.

96
00:06:10,314 --> 00:06:15,014
An hour in a B-52 costs less than an hour
in a B-1 or a B-2.

97
00:06:15,530 --> 00:06:18,250
When the job is carrying missiles
to the edge of the map,

98
00:06:18,330 --> 00:06:20,390
what matters isn't being hard to see.

99
00:06:21,110 --> 00:06:23,370
It's how much you carry, and how far.

100
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:27,870
But here's the strange part.

101
00:06:28,730 --> 00:06:33,370
A lot of what's inside a B-52 today
didn't exist in 1962.

102
00:06:34,287 --> 00:06:38,147
Radios, navigation,
computers, cockpit screens, weapons.

103
00:06:38,927 --> 00:06:41,367
All replaced, some of them more than once.

104
00:06:42,095 --> 00:06:46,855
A new radar is being fitted, based
on the radar of a modern fighter jet.

105
00:06:47,367 --> 00:06:48,907
And next come the engines.

106
00:06:49,827 --> 00:06:53,827
The ones it flies on now
were designed in the 1950s.

107
00:06:54,254 --> 00:06:58,094
They're being replaced by Rolls-Royce
engines from the same family

108
00:06:58,223 --> 00:07:00,563
that powers Gulfstream business jets.

109
00:07:01,327 --> 00:07:02,927
Eight old engines out.

110
00:07:03,007 --> 00:07:04,647
Eight new ones in.

111
00:07:05,265 --> 00:07:07,865
Why not four bigger ones,
like a modern airliner?

112
00:07:08,753 --> 00:07:11,773
Because that would turn
an engine swap into a redesign.

113
00:07:12,603 --> 00:07:14,063
Losing one big engine on take-off

114
00:07:14,293 --> 00:07:18,623
would pull the plane hard to one side,
so it would need a new rudder.

115
00:07:19,023 --> 00:07:23,103
It would also need new cockpit controls,
changes to the wing,

116
00:07:23,231 --> 00:07:25,231
and years of new flight tests.

117
00:07:26,051 --> 00:07:28,871
Eight smaller engines avoid most of that.

118
00:07:31,281 --> 00:07:33,661
The upgraded plane gets a new letter.

119
00:07:34,221 --> 00:07:35,421
The B-52J.

120
00:07:36,413 --> 00:07:39,953
Its test flights are planned
for the end of the 2020s.

121
00:07:42,192 --> 00:07:47,412
Meanwhile, the Air Force is buying a
brand-new stealth bomber, the B-21 Raider.

122
00:07:47,891 --> 00:07:51,211
And the long-term plan
is a bomber fleet of just two types.

123
00:07:51,912 --> 00:07:53,612
Not the B-1. Not the B-2.

124
00:07:54,717 --> 00:07:56,737
The newest bomber, and the oldest.

125
00:07:57,608 --> 00:07:59,348
One gets in, unseen.

126
00:08:00,179 --> 00:08:02,619
The other carries a lot, from far away.

127
00:08:05,569 --> 00:08:08,289
So why is the B-52 still flying?

128
00:08:09,614 --> 00:08:12,434
Not because it was the best bomber
ever built.

129
00:08:13,123 --> 00:08:16,863
It was never the fastest,
the highest, or the hardest to see.

130
00:08:17,486 --> 00:08:20,106
Its replacements were built
around a technology.

131
00:08:21,038 --> 00:08:23,718
The B-52 was built around a job.

132
00:08:24,398 --> 00:08:28,318
The technology kept expiring.
The job never did.

133
00:08:31,176 --> 00:08:35,776
And everything that did go out of date,
the engines, the electronics, the weapons,

134
00:08:36,041 --> 00:08:38,381
turned out to be the parts you could swap.

135
00:08:39,103 --> 00:08:43,983
What's left of the 1948 design
is the part that's hardest to replace.

136
00:08:44,724 --> 00:08:47,784
A big, strong wing
that carries a lot, a long way.

137
00:08:50,684 --> 00:08:52,664
So here's a question for you.

138
00:08:53,519 --> 00:08:55,759
What do you think will finally
ground the B-52?

139
00:08:56,913 --> 00:09:00,823
A better plane, or a job it can't do?
Let me know in the comments.
